my talk of the table is Kareem  abdul-jabbar and his response he's one  of the greatest basketball players of  all time his response to what's happened  the NBA's all-time leading scorer has  also been a social and political  activist throughout his career he  boycotted the 1968 Olympic Games and  supported Muhammad Ali's decision to  refuse to fight in the Vietnam War in a  very powerful op-ed that was published  Saturday in the Los Angeles Times the  best-selling author defended the  nationwide protests against police  brutality first on CBS this morning  we're very happy to say that Kareem  abdul-jabbar joins us from his home in  Newport Beach California it's really  good to see you Kareem abdul-jabbar let  me just say your your op-ed brought  tears to my eyes it the headline is  don't understand the protests what  you're seeing is people push to the edge  and I had to stop for a second and take  that in and I love how you started the  op-ed you talk about the differences  between the way black people and white  people may be perceiving this including  the Central Park jogger case the Central  Park woman can you just give a couple of  examples in the point you were trying to  make here well I think I can ask a  question and I think that would really  crystallize everything everybody I think  remembers what Colin Kaepernick went  through and his protest  was a peaceful protest about this very  issue and he was ostracized he lost his  job and he was blackballed for it that  was peaceful protest that's what it got  him that that's the benefit that it got  him and nothing has changed since then  think about this nothing has changed  since what was supposed to be a routine  traffic stop of Rodney King that was 30  years ago and still nothing has changed  white cops still can act with impunity  and and killed people that they feel  like they want to kill it's it's got to  stop someplace and powerless people have  no voice  a lot of them are losing their  opportunity to vote because the  Republicans are working very hard to  limit voter participation so what tools  do these people have to affect change  think about that means to change they  can't accept that I do think for the  first time a lot of people are thinking  about it you say black protesters are  people pushed to the edge not because  they want bars and nails so nail salons  open they just want to live and to  breathe racism in America you said it's  like dust in the air explain what you  mean by that well it's just like if you  have you ever been in a room and there's  something you feel a little something  it's in your nose there's something in  the air a dust or pollen that you can't  see but if you take a flashlight and  shine it to the air you see all these  little dust motes in the air and little  particles of things racism is like that  it's it's ingrained in our society and  it's taken for granted and all of the  things that are taken for granted can  accumulate and be deadly on certain  segments of the population and that has  and it comes down on the heads of poor  people and people of color you say for  those who say the looters are hurting  the cause you said you're not wrong but  you're also not right and I think it's  so important Kareem to make the  distinction between for the most part  these protests are peaceful but where a  lot of attention is focused on the  looting and the burning of the police  cars and I just don't want the message  to be lost about what is at stake here  and what people are really talking about  so you say you're not wrong but you're  not right either yeah I think we have to  look at it that way because these are  people who do not have any opportunity  to to have their voice heard and have  someone react to it and with respect and  appreciation of what they're saying they  do not have that power they're powerless  and  I just I have this vision of a sign that  I saw in Minneapolis that said can you  hear us now  that got to me was very significant I  mean that I think that's what it's about  we remember in 1968 when you boycotted  the Olympics what do you think that what  do you think this current generation can  learn from what you experienced what  differences do you see well I think that  this current generation has to  understand that they have to cross the  finish line with what needs to be done  and that's going to take a lot of  determination in organization are they  gonna have to get out and vote and make  their voices heard in a way that can  affect change you know violence and  looting and burning might get you some  attention but it's not going to change  anything  significantly so people that are doing  that really have to realize that and  they have to step up and organize and  both yeah killer Mike was very very  powerful over the weekend where he said  anger is not a strategy the mayor of  Atlanta said protests you need a plan  you can't just start marching in the  streets it sounds like you're saying the  same thing too you need to have a  strategy you need to have a plan we have  to have a plan and we have to engage our  fellow citizens and get them to do  what's right because too many times we  find out too late that the American  people are behind law and order in a way  that can benefit all Americans that  that's not a foreign subject to them but  they don't understand the difference  between what they experienced and what  the average person of color experiences  and that that needs to be underlined at  this point I love how you ended the  peace Kareem you said you do not want to  rush to judgment you want to rush to  justice what does justice look like to  you  oh well I think justice will look like  maybe reinforcing the consent decrees  that have been sent out to a number of  police departments to change their  culture and trying to get involved in  the escalation the Supreme Court is very  tough on people who try to get counts  get cops to be held accountable I think  you know a lot of those decisions we  have to find a legal way to deal with  cops with bad cops you know the good  cops the overwhelming majority of cops  are good cops and they need to be  protected and you know in all of this -  because they have a very important job  so you know we you know we got a lot of  work to do  and all of us have to be infinite every  American yeah and I think it's also  important to point out that most police  officers men and women are really  dedicated to their job and want to do  the right thing and most of them do the  right thing what we don't want is for  people to start vilifying cops and all  of this and you see the police coming  out to support the protesters and  numbers that most people have never seen  before I think that's important it's  absolutely important girl it's something  that has to be done and all of us all  Americans have to come together and and  and push this make make something happen  it's so clearly in front of us that this  is a this issue has to be dealt with I  hope things are different this time I  want to thank you Kareem abdul-jabbar  for your time and may I just say this  has nothing to do with the interview you  have one of the best decorated  backgrounds in your home I've ever seen  it's very pretty  so Bravo to you and your decorating team  whoever that is it's beautifully  beautifully done I'm gonna call and get  some tips on that thank you sir
 

